WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.959
Film Courage: I’m just curious, what were
some common clichés (at that time) which

00:00:02.959 --> 00:00:05.240
you were seeing in these scripts?

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.940
Daniel Calvisi: Well some big mistakes I would
say that I would get from scripts coming in

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:19.170
the door were big really dense blocks of description,
just too many words on the page.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:24.400
The worst is when they make the margins really
small so they can fit more on the page because

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.949
they haven’t really tightened the script
up enough.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:36.120
So with making the margins tiny and even the
spacing, you can make tighter in final draft

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.370
and in other screenwriting software, if you
do that the [script] reader immediately knows

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.570
you are cheating and they know that your script
may say it’s a 110 pages.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.840
But if you went to normal margins and normal
spacing it would probably be like 123 pages

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.910
or something.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.450
So readers hate that.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.149
You know right off the bat because you are
taking up more of their time and it also shows

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:05.409
that you’re an undisciplined writer who
doesn’t know how to write a well-paced script

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:11.810
in the proper spacing and format with let’s
say the average script being around 105 to

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:15.340
110 pages these days.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:21.060
So there is that and then it takes too long
to get to the inciting incident.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.069
The inciting incident should be between page
8 to 10.

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:29.290
Sometimes 12 is okay but we really need a
catalyst, you know?

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.020
We need an inciting incident to happen.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:39.990
And then as well later on you don’t want
Act 1 to be too far into the script.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.960
The break of Act 1 and Act 2 should be probably
around page 30.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.710
It doesn’t have to be exactly on page 30,
but around page 30.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:52.909
But in some beginner scripts it would literally
be on page 47 before a really big turn in

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:57.630
direction happened and it felt like we were
in Act two.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.630
So those are some examples.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.709
However the on-the-nose dialogue could be
one of the worst.

00:02:02.709 --> 00:02:08.679
I like to say sometimes talking heads dialogue
where it’s just two characters talking to

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:10.899
each other (kind of at each other).

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:12.659
And then Q&amp;A sessions are the worst.

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:16.299
That’s where one character keeps asking
another character questions and then they

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:17.739
just keep answering it.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:20.110
So it’s almost like a interview.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:25.129
And if that goes on for too long it really
tires out the reader and it gets really what

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:25.749
we call on-the-nose.

